.TH BUFFER_DUMMYREAD 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
buffer_dummyread \- Dummy read function for buffer operations

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/buffer.h>

.IB ssize_t
.IB buffer_dummyread (fd_type fd, void* buf, size_t len, buffer* b);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBbuffer_dummyread\fR function is a dummy read function used in buffer operations. It simply returns 0, indicating that no data was read.

The function takes the following parameters:
.IB fd_type
The file descriptor for the buffer.
.IB void*
The buffer to store the read data.
.IB size_t
The length of the buffer to store the read data.
.IB buffer*
A pointer to the buffer structure.

The \fBbuffer_dummyread\fR function is intended to be used as a placeholder for a real read function when no data needs to be read from the buffer.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBbuffer_dummyread\fR function in a buffer operation:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/buffer.h>

int main() {
    buffer* b = buffer_new();
    fd_type fd = open("example.txt", O_RDONLY, 0644);
    if(fd < 0) {
        perror("open");
        buffer_free(b);
        return 1;
    }

    char buffer[1024];
    ssize_t n = buffer_read(fd, buffer, sizeof(buffer), b, &buffer_dummyread);
    if(n < 0) {
        perror("buffer_read");
        close(fd);
        buffer_free(b);
        return 1;
    }

    close(fd);
    buffer_free(b);
    return 0;
}
.nef